// Streaming a zstd frame — the file `@/packages/gzip`'s README said would find nothing new.
//
// > **`zstd` has the same shape** … Everything here applies to it unchanged, and it is not rewritten
// > because it would find nothing new.
//
// The part that claim is about is right: `packages/zstd/src/stream.wac` traps on `Read.Failed` with
// no `broken` equivalent, exactly as gzip does, and gzip's argument carries over word for word.
//
// It is the third prediction of this kind tested today and the third that was wrong, because the
// file has two difficulties gzip has not got and its own header names them both:
//
// > **The block is the unit.** A compressed block carries its own literals section and FSE tables and
// > nothing in it can be decoded until all of it has arrived …
// >
// > **The window is declared, not fixed.** DEFLATE reaches back 32 KiB; zstd reaches back as far as
// > the frame header says … Output is therefore retained until it is `windowSize` behind and only
// > then written away.
//
// The second one is the finding, and it is not about zstd.

import { Result, Bytes, AsyncGenerator } from "core";
import { Buf } from "@/packages/bytes";

/**
 * Why a frame stopped, in `@/packages/gzip`'s shape because the shape is the point.
 *
 * That package splits `union<SourceFailed, Corrupt>` on *whether a caller may retry*, and its README
 * is right that the split carries over unchanged here — which is the half of its prediction that
 * held. Declared in this file rather than a `fault.wac` of its own, because two members and a
 * borrowed argument do not need one.
 */
export struct SourceFailed { }
/** The frame is not zstd, or not this zstd. Eight members in gzip; elided here. */
export struct Corrupt { }
export union<SourceFailed, Corrupt> Fault;

/**
 * How far back into the output a match reaches.
 *
 * **A distance, not an index — and that is the whole of what this file changes.**
 *
 * `packages/bytes`'s `Buf.pushRepeat(i32 at, i32 count)` takes an *absolute* position, and
 * `packages/zstd/src/sequences.wac` calls it as `out.pushRepeat(out.len - offset, …)`. The streaming
 * decoder then calls `dropFront` to release output that is more than `windowSize` behind, which
 * shifts every byte down and reduces `len` by the same amount — so `out.len - offset` still names
 * the same byte afterwards. `stream.wac`'s header states exactly that:
 *
 * > That works without touching the match code because `sequences.wac` copies with
 * > `out.pushRepeat(out.len - offset, …)` — an index computed from the *current* length — so
 * > dropping the front of the buffer moves the base under it and every offset still lands on the
 * > same byte.
 *
 * It is correct and it is **an invariant across two packages held by a sentence**. `Buf` offers an
 * operation that invalidates absolute indices and an operation that takes one, and nothing in either
 * signature relates them. A caller that computed `at` once and reused it across a `dropFront` would
 * read the wrong bytes, silently, and produce output that is wrong only for inputs with a match
 * older than the window — which is to say, only for large ones.
 *
 * `FromEnd` cannot be invalidated by `dropFront`, because it does not name a position. That is the
 * same move as `@/packages/webrtc`'s `Tsn`: a scalar whose meaning is relative, spelled as though it
 * were absolute, where the type is the only place the distinction can live.
 */
export const struct FromEnd {
  i32 back;
}

/**
 * Input arriving in pieces, presented as a window the decoder can index into.
 *
 * **The amount to wait for is always known exactly** — a block's size is in its three-byte header —
 * so this is not *read until you have enough*. It is *read this many*, and the type can say so.
 * `packages/zstd`'s `Pull` accumulates against a target held in a local; making the target the
 * parameter is the difference between a loop that might terminate and a loop that says how.
 */
export struct Pull {
  Buf held;
  /** Exactly `n` bytes, or the source ended first. */
  async Result<Bytes, Fault> exactly(this, i32 n) { … }
}

/** A frame in, its bytes out, retaining only what the declared window still needs. */
export async gen<Bytes> Result<void, union<E, Fault>> unzstd<E>(
  AsyncGenerator<Bytes, Result<void, E>> source,
) { … }

// ## 1. The invariant is `Buf`'s, and zstd is where it happens to be load-bearing
//
// `Buf` has eleven ways to add and two ways to remove — `dropFront`, and `truncate` if
// `issues/system/0333a` is taken. `dropFront` moves the contents and `pushRepeat` takes a position
// into them. Every other caller in the tree computes its position and uses it immediately, so the
// hazard has one witness and the witness is correct.
//
// That is the ordinary shape of an invariant nobody has written: **not a bug, and not safe either —
// safe because of what the callers happen to do.** The count matters here. One caller holds it, in
// another package, and the sentence that explains it is in a third file from the one that would
// break.
//
// Two fixes, and the cheap one is not the type:
//
//   * **`FromEnd`**, above. `pushRepeat(FromEnd, i32)` cannot be handed a stale index because there
//     are no indices. Costs a wrapper on the one caller and removes the hazard for every future one.
//   * **A comment on `dropFront` saying it invalidates positions.** Free, and it is where a reader
//     looking at `Buf` would find it — which is not where the current sentence is.
//
// The second is what a careful person does and the first is what makes the next caller safe. This
// directory has argued for the first eight times; the honest note is that here the second would
// probably have been enough, because there is one caller and it is right.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`Pull.exactly(n)` cannot say that `n` came from a header this decoder just read.** The block size
// is three bytes off the wire, so it is attacker-controlled, and `exactly` will happily be asked for
// two gigabytes. The shipped code bounds it with `blockMaxSize` from `frame.wac` — a check at the
// call site, one call site, in a package whose whole subject is untrusted input. A `BlockSize` that
// can only be built by validating against the frame header is the type, and it is
// `@/packages/http`'s *checked* problem again: the value is fine when it is made and the check is
// somewhere else.
//
// **The declared window may exceed memory and nothing says what happens.** `windowSize` comes from
// the frame header and may be megabytes; the decoder retains that much output. A frame declaring a
// gigabyte window is legal zstd and this is a program that will try. The shipped file does not
// mention it, `Fault` has no member for it, and the honest answer is a limit the *caller* sets —
// which is a parameter this signature does not have and `@/packages/gzip`'s does not either.
//
// **The retained output is both a buffer and a window, and it is one `Buf`.** Bytes ahead of the
// window are output waiting to be yielded; bytes behind it are history the matcher still needs. One
// length, two meanings, and `dropFront` is the operation that moves the boundary. Two types would
// separate them and would need the history to be indexable from the buffer, which is the thing a
// single `Buf` gets for free — so this is a case where the fused representation is doing real work
// and splitting it would cost a copy. Recorded rather than proposed, and it is the second time in
// two days that the flat thing was right: `@/packages/http`'s headers were the first.
